    "traits": "• You like to build or assemble thingsn  • You like to analyze and interpret given informationn  • You like to lead and influence othersn  • You like to solve complex problemsn  • You are detail• oriented and structuredn  • You have a strong sense of empathy for othersn  • You are a patient teacher and persuasive communicatorn  • You are adaptable to evolving technologiesn  • You possess professional curiosity and a mindset for advocacy",
    "courseFees": "Fees for accessibility training and certification vary by provider and membership status. IAAP certification exams, such as the Web Accessibility Specialist (WAS) and Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC), typically range from $455 to $495 for members and $555 to $595 for non• members. Specialized training courses from providers like Deque or Princeton University often range from free to $400, while comprehensive professional diplomas can cost between $2,350 and $4,020. Additionally, many platforms like Coursera, W3C, and AudioEye offer free foundational courses and digital accessibility training.",
    "scholarships": "• IAAP Professional Certification Scholarship: Provides financial hardship• based scholarships for certifications like CPACC, WAS, and ADS for accessibility professionals.n  • DARE Academy Scholarship Fund: Offers free access to IAAP education materials and CPACC examination costs for individuals engaged in digital accessibility advocacy.n  • Google Lime Scholarship: Available for students with disabilities studying computing or computer science.n  • Microsoft Disability Scholarship: Supports high school seniors with disabilities who are pursuing a career in technology.n  • Johnson & Johnson Access• Ability Lime Scholarship: Awarded to students with disabilities pursuing degrees in STEM or healthcare.n  • Frederick J. Krause Scholarship on Health and Disability: For students with disabilities majoring in public health, disability studies, health promotion, or related fields.n  • Barbara Greenberg Distinguished Scholarship: Provides financial support to undergraduate students pursuing specialized coursework in Disability Studies at the University of Washington.n  • Joy Zabala Fellowship: Supports early career professionals to strengthen expertise in assistive technology (AT) and accessible educational materials (AEM).n  • Susie Elizabeth Memorial Scholarship: For students who plan to work with individuals with autism in careers such as speech therapy or special education.n  • J.E. Anderson Family College Scholarship: For first• generation students pursuing a field of study in special education.n  • Hue Ta Asian American Scholarship: Supports young leaders championing mental health and disability rights.n  • Everyday Champion Awards: Awards $5,000 to K• 12 professionals who help students with learning disabilities thrive.",
    "educationPath": "1.  Complete 10+2 in any stream, though Science or Commerce with a focus on Computer Science, IT, or Design is beneficial.n2.  Complete a Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Human• Computer Interaction, Web Design, or Disability Studies.n    ORn    Complete a Bachelor's degree in a related field like Education, Instructional Technology, or Visual Communication.n3.  Optional: Complete a Master's degree in Human• Computer Interaction, Web Development, Accessibility Studies, or UX Design for advanced knowledge.n4.  Gain 1• 3 years of practical experience through internships or entry• level roles in web development, UX design, or quality assurance focusing on accessibility features.n5.  Obtain professional certifications such as Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) or Web Accessibility Specialist (WAS) from the International Association of Accessibility Professionals (IAAP).n6.  Maintain certifications by earning continuing education credits every three years through workshops, webinars, and research.",
