    "educationPath": "1.  Complete a high school diploma or GED, ideally with a focus on science and math.n2.  Attend a technical school or community college to earn a postsecondary certificate or diploma in wind energy technology. This typically takes 7 to 12 months.n    ORn    Complete an Associate of Applied Science Degree in wind turbine technology or a related field like electrical or mechanical engineering technology.n3.  Obtain industry• standard certifications such as Global Wind Organization (GWO) Basic Safety Training (BST) and Basic Technical Training (BTT). Additional certifications may include tower climbing, self• rescue, and CPR/First Aid.n4.  Participate in on• the• job training provided by an employer or manufacturer, which can last up to one year and focuses on specific turbine models.n5.  Consider an apprenticeship to gain advanced mechanical repair experience and improve employability.For admissions to specific programs, check individual institution requirements. Some may require a physical exam and a minimum grade in high school algebra.",
