    "workLocation": "Places of Work: On• site at wind farms, including offshore and rural locations; turbine nacelles, hubs, and rotors; wind field substations.nnWork Environment: Technicians generally work outdoors, often at great heights (over 200 feet) and in confined spaces. They may work in extreme temperatures and variable weather conditions. Travel is frequently required to various worksites, and offshore assignments may involve being away from home for several days or weeks. Safety equipment, such as fall• protection harnesses, is mandatory due to the height of the towers.",
    "studyLocation": "This list of institutes is indicative onlynnGOVERNMENT INSTITUTES1.  Gloucester County Institute of Technology (GCIT), New Jerseyn2.  Salem County Vocational Technical School (SCVTS), New Jerseyn3.  Camden County Vocational Technical Schools (CCVTS), New JerseyPRIVATE INSTITUTES1.  North American Wind Academy (NAWA), Fort Worth, Texasn2.  Universal Technical Institute (UTI), Canton, MI; Lisle, IL; Rancho Cucamonga, CA; Dallas/Fort Worth, TX; Houston, TXn3.  Centura College, Virginian4.  Pinnacle Career Institute (PCI), Online with 10• day session in Kansas City, Missourin5.  Energy Innovation AS, Egersund, Norwayn6.  Global Wind Service Academyn7.  Danish Wind Power AcademyCOMMUNITY COLLEGES1.  Camden County College (CCC), New Jerseyn2.  Atlantic Cape Community College, New Jerseyn3.  Rowan College of South Jersey (RCSJ), New Jersey",
